WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.458
YURI GRINSHTEYN:
The ancient Greeks

00:00:01.458 --> 00:00:03.660
told of how Prometheus
stole fire from the gods

00:00:03.660 --> 00:00:05.400
and brought it to humankind.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:07.920
Today, Prometheus is
bringing better observability

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:10.890
to Kubernetes operators as a
leading open source monitoring

00:00:10.890 --> 00:00:11.850
framework.

00:00:11.850 --> 00:00:15.240
In this episode, we'll look at
how to set up Prometheus on GKE

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:17.520
and use it to instrument
your application with custom

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:18.370
metrics.

00:00:18.370 --> 00:00:19.680
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:23.019
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:28.500
Last time, we looked at how to
use OpenCensus to add custom

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.970
metrics to our application.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.640
Today, we'll do the
same with Prometheus.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.980
Before we start, a
bit of background.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.650
Prometheus is an open source
systems monitoring and alerting

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.040
tool kit originally
built at SoundCloud.

00:00:40.040 --> 00:00:42.660
It has a very active
developer and user community

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:46.470
and joined the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation in 2016.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:48.750
So how do you set it up?

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.090
The fastest and easiest
way to actually set up

00:00:51.090 --> 00:00:53.130
Prometheus in your
GKE cluster is

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:57.060
to use Helm, which is a
package manager for Kubernetes.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:58.970
Once you have Helm
installed and configured,

00:00:58.970 --> 00:01:00.720
you can install
Prometheus in your cluster

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:02.670
simply by running
the helm install

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.664
stable/prometheus command.

00:01:04.664 --> 00:01:07.200
The install will create
multiple components--

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:10.620
the Prometheus server itself, an
exporter running on every node

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:13.140
to capture node
statistics, alert manager

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:15.810
to send email notifications,
and the push gateway

00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.360
to accept metrics to be
pushed to Prometheus.

00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:21.010
We'll primarily interact
with the server.

00:01:21.010 --> 00:01:23.790
Let's validate the installation
by port forwarding the server

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.650
pod to our local host.

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.380
This allows us to access
the Prometheus UI and graph

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.440
metrics to confirm that data
collection and persistence are

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.280
working.

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:33.880
Now that Prometheus
is installed,

00:01:33.880 --> 00:01:36.090
we are ready to start adding
metric instrumentation

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:37.710
to our application.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.380
But first, let's quickly
review the kinds of metrics

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.220
that Prometheus supports.

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.490
A counter is a
cumulative metric that

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:48.030
represents a single increasing
value that can only increase

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.303
or be reset to 0 and restart.

00:01:50.303 --> 00:01:51.720
For example, you
can use a counter

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:54.330
to represent the number
of requests served, tasks

00:01:54.330 --> 00:01:56.190
completed, or errors.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.350
A gauge is a metric
that represents

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:02.120
a single numerical value that
can arbitrarily go up and down.

00:02:02.120 --> 00:02:04.200
Gauges are typically
used for measured values,

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:06.790
like temperatures or
current memory usage,

00:02:06.790 --> 00:02:10.930
but also counts, like the
number of concurrent requests.

00:02:10.930 --> 00:02:13.920
A histogram samples
observations and counts them

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:15.540
in configurable buckets.

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:18.680
It also provides a sum
of all observed values.

00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.580
Histograms are most
often used to measure

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:23.700
the distributions of things like
request durations or response

00:02:23.700 --> 00:02:25.650
sizes.

00:02:25.650 --> 00:02:29.160
Finally, a summary samples
observations and provides

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.470
a total count and a sum
of all observed values.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:34.560
It also calculates
configurable quantiles

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:36.840
over a sliding time window.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:39.450
Summaries are usually used to
values where the total value is

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.748
important, like payload sizes.

00:02:41.748 --> 00:02:43.290
Now that we know
what kind of metrics

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.040
we can expose in
our application,

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:47.820
it's important to understand
how Prometheus itself works.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.170
It's a basic pull model.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.660
Our application needs to expose
a dedicated endpoint where

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.880
the metrics will be
available, and we

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.800
need to tell Prometheus
where that endpoint is

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.140
and how frequently to
scrape it for metrics,

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:00.450
so let's do both of those now.

00:03:00.450 --> 00:03:02.930
First, let's instrument
our application.

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:04.565
We'll create a
simple Go application

00:03:04.565 --> 00:03:06.940
and expose a gauge metric in it.

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:07.920
Here's the code.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.830
You can find a link to the
repo in the video description.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.500
The first step is to define
the metric itself as a gauge

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:15.780
and provide its name
and a description.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:17.610
Next, we create a
function that will

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:19.380
set the value of our metric.

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.780
In our example, we're just
generating a random value

00:03:21.780 --> 00:03:24.450
so that we know it'll
change every time.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.040
Now we can run our application.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:27.540
When we access the
metrics endpoint,

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:30.150
we can see that our custom
metric is generated and shown

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:31.330
here.

00:03:31.330 --> 00:03:33.060
Now our application
is instrumented

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.010
with a custom metric, which is
being exported to Prometheus.

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.653
We need to tell Prometheus
where to find this information.

00:03:38.653 --> 00:03:41.070
We'll do that by adding some
annotations in the deployment

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.220
spec in our application before
we deploy it to Kubernetes.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.440
We'll enable scraping
and define the port

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:49.770
and endpoint where the
metrics are available.

00:03:49.770 --> 00:03:52.140
Our last step is to set up
Stackdriver integration,

00:03:52.140 --> 00:03:54.885
to have this metric be
ingested into Stackdriver.

00:03:54.885 --> 00:03:56.510
We'll install the
Stackdriver collector

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:59.280
on our cluster, which will
patch the Prometheus server

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.370
deployment.

00:04:00.370 --> 00:04:02.240
Now when we go to
Metrics Explorer,

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:04.890
we can see our random
value metric there.

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:07.500
I hope that you've seen how
Prometheus is a fantastic way

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.030
to add custom metric
instrumentation

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:11.560
to your application and have
those metrics be ingested

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:12.675
in Stackdriver.

00:04:12.675 --> 00:04:14.550
Thank you for joining
me today, and come back

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.040
next time when we explore
options for managing monitoring

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:18.329
and logging costs.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:19.589
That's it for this episode.

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:20.850
This is "The Stack Doctor."

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:21.900
Stay healthy out there.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:25.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

